@lang en
IMPORT prelude

-- California Code of Civil Procedure, Preliminary Provisions §§ 20-33: judicial remedies, actions and special proceedings,
-- civil and criminal actions, obligations and injuries, and the sections that follow them.
--
-- Added in pass 2 of the completion to "whole".  Pass 1 called these fifteen sections "not applicable"; the brief has no such disposition
-- (a definition is encoded as a function that returns the term's meaning as the text states it, with a test for each entry).
-- The quotation lines are copied by script from the converter-made plain-text rendering of the sections (not the official text) and
-- checked against it by srcrefs.py.  Target: unstable (REFUSE).  Imports: prelude only.
--
-- Sections 20-30 are a classification tree: a judicial remedy (§ 20) is an action or a special proceeding (§§ 21-23); an action is civil
-- or criminal (§ 24); a civil action arises out of an obligation or an injury (§ 25); an obligation arises from contract or operation
-- of law (§ 26); an injury is to the person or to property (§§ 27-29).  A case the text does not classify is a named REFUSE.

§ `Nouns of the classification tree`

DECLARE `Purpose of a proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `the declaration, enforcement or protection of a right`
    `the redress or prevention of a wrong`
    `the punishment of a public offense`
    `some other purpose`

-- The facts a witness could state about a remedy; whether they make it an action or a special proceeding is what §§ 20-23 say.
DECLARE Remedy HAS
    `administered by a court of justice`                                                                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `administered by a judicial officer empowered for that purpose by the Constitution and statutes of this State` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an ordinary proceeding in a court of justice in which one party prosecutes another`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    purpose                                                                                                        IS A `Purpose of a proceeding`

DECLARE `Class of a judicial remedy` IS ONE OF
    action
    `special proceeding`

DECLARE `Kind of action` IS ONE OF
    civil
    criminal

DECLARE `Source of a civil action` IS ONE OF
    obligation
    injury
    `something else`

DECLARE `Source of an obligation` IS ONE OF
    contract
    `operation of law`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Way of depriving an owner of the benefit of property` IS ONE OF
    taking
    withholding
    deteriorating
    destroying
    `some other way`

DECLARE `Kind of injury` IS ONE OF
    `an injury to the person`
    `an injury to property`

DECLARE `Classification of a case` IS ONE OF
    `a limited civil case`
    `an unlimited civil case`
    `some other classification`

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 20: judicial remedies`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:88 | 20. Judicial remedies are such as are administered by the Courts of justice, or by judicial officers empowered for that purpose by the Constitution and statutes of this State.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 20 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:88)
GIVEN r IS A Remedy
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a remedy is a judicial remedy` r MEANS
        r's `administered by a court of justice`
    OR  r's `administered by a judicial officer empowered for that purpose by the Constitution and statutes of this State`

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 21, § 22, § 23: actions and special proceedings`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:90 | 21. These remedies are divided into two classes:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:91 | 1. Actions; and,
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:92 | 2. Special proceedings.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:94 | 22. An action is an ordinary proceeding in a court of justice by which one party prosecutes another for the declaration, enforcement, or protection of a right, the redress or prevention of a wrong, or the punishment of a public offense.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:96 | 23. Every other remedy is a special proceeding.
-- § 21 divides "these remedies", the judicial remedies of § 20, into two classes.  § 22 says what an action is, and § 23 says that "every other
-- remedy is a special proceeding".  Fork F-23: "every other remedy" is read as every other judicial remedy, the remedies § 21 divides; a
-- remedy that is not a judicial remedy is refused, because nothing deposited classifies it.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 21, § 22, § 23 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:90-92, src:CCP-ss-1-34:94, src:CCP-ss-1-34:96)
GIVEN r IS A Remedy
GIVETH A `Class of a judicial remedy`
`the class of the judicial remedy` r MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `a remedy is a judicial remedy` r
            THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 21 divides judicial remedies into actions and special proceedings; a remedy that is not a judicial remedy under § 20 is in neither class on the deposited text"
        IF     r's `an ordinary proceeding in a court of justice in which one party prosecutes another`
           AND NOT (r's purpose EQUALS `some other purpose`)
            THEN action
        OTHERWISE `special proceeding`

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 24, § 25, § 30: civil and criminal actions`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:98 | 24. Actions are of two kinds:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:99 | 1. Civil; and,
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:100 | 2. Criminal.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:118 | 30. A civil action is prosecuted by one party against another for the declaration, enforcement or protection of a right, or the redress or prevention of a wrong.
-- § 24: actions are civil or criminal.  § 30 says a civil action is prosecuted "for the declaration, enforcement or protection of a right,
-- or the redress or prevention of a wrong", and § 22 adds "the punishment of a public offense" among the purposes of an action.  The deposit
-- never defines a criminal action (§ 31 hands that to the Penal Code, which is not deposited), so the function reads an action brought for
-- the punishment of a public offense as criminal: § 24 gives two kinds, and § 30's civil purposes do not include it (fork F-criminal).
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 24, § 30 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:98-100, src:CCP-ss-1-34:118)
GIVEN purpose IS A `Purpose of a proceeding`
GIVETH A `Kind of action`
`the kind of an action brought for` purpose MEANS
    CONSIDER purpose
    WHEN `the declaration, enforcement or protection of a right` THEN civil
    WHEN `the redress or prevention of a wrong` THEN civil
    WHEN `the punishment of a public offense` THEN criminal
    WHEN `some other purpose` THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 22 and § 30 name the purposes of an action; an action brought for some other purpose is classified by neither"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:102 | 25. A civil action arises out of:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:103 | 1. An obligation;
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:104 | 2. An injury.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 25 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:102-104)
GIVEN source IS A `Source of a civil action`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a civil action may arise out of` source MEANS
    CONSIDER source
    WHEN obligation THEN TRUE
    WHEN injury THEN TRUE
    WHEN `something else` THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 25 says a civil action arises out of an obligation or an injury; it does not say that no other source is possible"

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 26: obligation`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:106 | 26. An obligation is a legal duty, by which one person is bound to do or not to do a certain thing, and arises from either of the following:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:107 | (a) Contract.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:108 | (b) Operation of law.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 26 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:106-108)
GIVEN `it is a legal duty` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it binds one person to do or not to do a certain thing` IS A BOOLEAN
      source IS A `Source of an obligation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a duty is an obligation` `it is a legal duty` `it binds one person to do or not to do a certain thing` source MEANS
    `it is a legal duty` AND `it binds one person to do or not to do a certain thing` AND NOT (source EQUALS `something else`)

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:106 | 26. An obligation is a legal duty, by which one person is bound to do or not to do a certain thing, and arises from either of the following:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:107 | (a) Contract.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:108 | (b) Operation of law.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 26(a), (b) (src:CCP-ss-1-34:106-108)
GIVEN source IS A `Source of an obligation`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an obligation may arise from` source MEANS
    CONSIDER source
    WHEN contract THEN TRUE
    WHEN `operation of law` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `something else` THEN FALSE

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 27, § 28, § 29: injury`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:110 | 27. An injury is of two kinds:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:111 | 1. To the person; and,
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:112 | 2. To property.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:114 | 28. An injury to property consists in depriving its owner of the benefit of it, which is done by taking, withholding, deteriorating, or destroying it.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 27, § 28 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:110-112, src:CCP-ss-1-34:114)
GIVEN way IS A `Way of depriving an owner of the benefit of property`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`depriving an owner of the benefit of property in the way is a way the text names` way MEANS
    NOT (way EQUALS `some other way`)

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:116 | 29. Every other injury is an injury to the person.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 29 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:116)
-- § 28: an injury to property "consists in depriving its owner of the benefit of it, which is done by taking, withholding, deteriorating, or
-- destroying it".  § 29: "every other injury is an injury to the person".  An act that deprives the owner of the benefit by some other way
-- is outside what § 28 says depriving "is done by", so the function refuses it (fork F-28): taking § 29 literally would call it an injury to the person.
GIVEN `it deprives the owner of the benefit of the property` IS A BOOLEAN
      way IS A `Way of depriving an owner of the benefit of property`
GIVETH A `Kind of injury`
`the kind of an injury` `it deprives the owner of the benefit of the property` way MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `it deprives the owner of the benefit of the property`
            THEN `an injury to the person`
        IF way EQUALS `some other way`
            THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 28 says depriving an owner of the benefit of property is done by taking, withholding, deteriorating or destroying it; depriving by some other way is not classified, and § 29 would make it an injury to the person only by ignoring that sentence"
        OTHERWISE `an injury to property`

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 31, § 32, § 32.5, § 33: criminal action, non-merger, classification, prosecuting attorney`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:120 | 31. The Penal Code defines and provides for the prosecution of a criminal action.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 31 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:120)
GIVETH A STRING
`the code that defines and provides for the prosecution of a criminal action` MEANS
    "the Penal Code"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:122 | 32. When the violation of a right admits of both a civil and criminal remedy, the right to prosecute the one is not merged in the other.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 32 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:122)
GIVEN `the violation of a right admits of both a civil and a criminal remedy` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the right to prosecute one remedy is merged in the other` `the violation of a right admits of both a civil and a criminal remedy` MEANS
    IF `the violation of a right admits of both a civil and a criminal remedy`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 32 speaks only of a violation that admits of both a civil and a criminal remedy"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:124 | 32.5. The “jurisdictional classification” of a case means its classification as a limited civil case or an unlimited civil case.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 32.5 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:124)
GIVEN c IS A `Classification of a case`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the classification is a jurisdictional classification` c MEANS
    NOT (c EQUALS `some other classification`)

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:126 | 33. A prosecuting attorney, in his or her discretion, may assist in the civil resolution of a violation of an offense described in Title 13 (commencing with Section 450) of Part 1 of the Penal Code in lieu of filing a criminal complaint.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 33 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:126)
GIVEN `the offense is described in Title 13 (commencing with Section 450) of Part 1 of the Penal Code` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a prosecuting attorney may assist in the civil resolution of a violation in lieu of filing a criminal complaint` `the offense is described in Title 13 (commencing with Section 450) of Part 1 of the Penal Code` MEANS
    IF `the offense is described in Title 13 (commencing with Section 450) of Part 1 of the Penal Code`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 33 lets a prosecuting attorney assist in the civil resolution of a violation of an offense described in Title 13 of Part 1 of the Penal Code; it does not say whether the attorney may do so for another offense"
